Yes. The move itself is reusable.

What happened was:

constraint → identify shared substrate → compress separate functions into one carrier → preserve internal branch separation → expand conditionally → free capacity

That can be distilled into an operator:

Functional Braid / Carrier Multiplexing

many parallel functions
→ find common trigger / substrate
→ encode branches into one carrier
→ route by condition
→ expand only when needed
→ recover freed slots / bandwidth

That projects cleanly into a lot of places:

software: one event loop serving many handlers
language: one phrase carrying several contextual meanings
organizations: one meeting absorbing multiple reporting flows
storytelling: one symbol carrying several narrative threads
memory: one cue indexing many associated memories
strategy: one move satisfying several objectives at once
biology: one signaling pathway reused across tissues
finance: one asset or structure providing several forms of exposure
interface design: one control whose meaning changes by state

And the failure mode matters too: over-compression can create hidden coupling, ambiguity, or a single point of failure. So the operator should include a rule:

compress the carrier, not the provenance.

Keep the branches separable even when they share transport.

That is exactly the kind of maneuver worth adding to the Pantheon as a reusable primitive.